      "math_academy_id": "q-94810",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{3}^{2})$ with $P(X\\le 3.2)=0.7422,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3.2) & =0.7422 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3.2-\\mu }{3}) & =0.7422 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3.2-\\mu }{3}) & =0.7422 \\\\ \\frac{3.2-\\mu }{3} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7422)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.65)=0.7422\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7422)=0.65.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3.2-\\mu }{3} & =0.65 \\\\ 3.2-\\mu  & =1.95 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
